6. White-matter tracts section (public/assets/wm/, public/assets/wm-manifest.json,
   src/data/generated/wm-manifest.json, src/data/generated/wm-connectome.json) is
   derived from the HCP-1065 population-averaged tractography atlas
   (https://brain.labsolver.org/hcp_trk_atlas.html; Yeh FC, Nat Commun
   2022;13:4933, doi:10.1038/s41467-022-32595-4). These files are not GPL-licensed:
   they are distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0
   International licence (CC BY-SA 4.0,
   https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/). Changes made: streamlines
   subsampled and resampled, the corpus callosum cut at the midline, files converted
   to compressed glTF; connection probabilities kept as published. The atlas is
   built from Human Connectome Project data, so the HCP terms and acknowledgement in
   section 5 apply to these files as well.

7. Skull base v1, the Z-Anatomy model (public/assets/skullbase/, public/assets/sb-manifest.json,
   src/data/generated/sb-*.json) is derived from Z-Anatomy
   (https://github.com/Z-Anatomy/Models-of-human-anatomy), which is based on
   BodyParts3D. These files are not GPL-licensed; they are distributed under the
   Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike licences of their sources:
     "BodyParts3D - The Database Center for Life Science - CC-BY-SA 2.1 Japan"
     "Z-Anatomy - The libre 3D atlas of anatomy - CC-BY-SA 4.0"
     Definitions: Wikipedia - CC-BY-SA 3.0 (each links its source page).
   Changes made: bones, soft tissue, vessels and cranial nerves of the head exported
   from the Z-Anatomy Blender file; coordinates converted to millimetres from the
   hypophysial fossa landmark; midline structures cut at the midline; bones
   smoothed by one subdivision level, decimated and shaded with baked ambient
   occlusion; vessels and nerves rebuilt as tubes with the radius Z-Anatomy models
   and cropped to the head; converted to compressed glTF; definitions shortened to
   their lead section.
   Mitsuhashi N, et al. BodyParts3D: 3D structure database for anatomical
   concepts. Nucleic Acids Res. 2009;37:D782-D785. doi:10.1093/nar/gkn613.

8. Cortex section (public/assets/cortex/, public/assets/ctx-manifest.json,
   src/data/generated/ctx-*.json) is derived from the MNI ICBM 2009b template
   surface and the FreeSurfer annotations (Destrieux 2009 aparc.a2009s,
   Desikan-Killiany aparc) distributed with Lead-DBS; like section 2 these files
   are GPL-3.0 and should not be used commercially. The annotations were made
   with FreeSurfer (https://surfer.nmr.mgh.harvard.edu/), whose licence
   (MGH, https://surfer.nmr.mgh.harvard.edu/fswiki/FreeSurferSoftwareLicense)
   is for research use and not for clinical applications. The definition texts in
   ctx-labels.json are Wikipedia leads shipped with Z-Anatomy, CC BY-SA 3.0, each
   linking its source page. Changes made: surfaces decimated and split by label,
   an inflated surface and sulcal-depth shading computed, converted to compressed
   glTF. Destrieux C, Fischl B, Dale A, Halgren E. NeuroImage. 2010;53:1-15.
   doi:10.1016/j.neuroimage.2010.06.010.

9. Spine section (public/assets/spine/, public/assets/spine-manifest.json,
   src/data/generated/spine-*.json) is derived from one CT of the VerSe 2020
   dataset (sub-verse823, https://osf.io/4skx2/), published under the Creative
   Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 licence; these files are not GPL-licensed
   but CC BY-SA 4.0 as well. VerSe asks that its three papers be cited:
     Löffler MT, et al. Radiol Artif Intell. 2020;2(4):e190138.
     Liebl H, et al. Sci Data. 2021;8(1):284.
     Sekuboyina A, et al. Med Image Anal. 2021;73:102166.
   Soft tissue, discs, the sacrum and pelvis were segmented from that CT with
   TotalSegmentator (open tasks, Apache-2.0; Wasserthal J, et al. Radiol Artif
   Intell. 2023;5(5):e230024). Changes made: bone surfaces taken from the CT at a
   fixed threshold within reach of the VerSe vertebra masks; segmentations turned
   into smoothed, decimated meshes split at the midline; schematic roots and psoas
   zones drawn by rule; coordinates moved to the centre of the L5 vertebral body;
   converted to compressed glTF.

10. Skull base v2 (public/assets/skullbase-ct/, public/assets/sbct-manifest.json,
   src/data/generated/sbct-*.json) is derived from one CT angiogram of the TopBrain
   2025 challenge data (case topcow_ct_011, University Hospital Zurich,
   https://topbrain2025.grand-challenge.org, https://zenodo.org/records/16878417),
   whose terms allow open use with the source given, non-commercially (commercial
   use needs the data owner's permission), and from Z-Anatomy (as in section 7,
   CC BY-SA). These files are not GPL-licensed. Changes made: the CT's bone surface
   taken at a fixed threshold within TotalSegmentator's skull mask, smoothed and
   decimated; TopBrain's vessel labels meshed; the CT moved rigidly into the
   Z-Anatomy frame; Z-Anatomy's nerves, unlabelled vessels, soft tissue and
   landmarks scaled and smoothly deformed onto the CT's skull; converted to
   compressed glTF.

11. Brainstem section (public/assets/brainstem/, public/assets/bs-manifest.json,
   src/data/generated/bs-*.json). The arousal network nuclei (bs_aan_*.glb) are
   derived from the Harvard Ascending Arousal Network Atlas 2.0 (Edlow BL, Kinney
   HC, 2023, Dryad, doi:10.5061/dryad.zw3r228d2), dedicated to the public domain
   (CC0 1.0). Everything else is derived from Z-Anatomy (as in section 7) and is
   distributed under CC BY-SA 4.0 (Z-Anatomy), CC BY-SA 2.1 Japan (BodyParts3D) and
   CC BY-SA 3.0 (Wikipedia definitions); these files are not GPL-licensed. Changes
   made: brainstem structures, cranial nerves and arteries exported from the
   Z-Anatomy Blender file and fitted into MNI space onto the ICBM 2009b T2 template
   (a similarity transform and a smooth deformation); nerves and arteries rebuilt as
   tubes; safe entry zones drawn by rule on the fitted surface; the AAN labels'
   surfaces taken after resampling; converted to compressed glTF. The section also
   shows the white-matter section's tracts (section 6) and the DBS section's deep
   nuclei (section 2) from their own files, under their own terms.

12. Skull base, the default model ("v3" in the pipeline; public/assets/skullbase-v3/,
   public/assets/sbv3-manifest.json,
   src/data/generated/sbv3-*.json) shows Skull base v2's CT bone and vessel files
   (section 10) with structures derived from the OpenEar library of human temporal
   bones (Sieber D, Erfurt P, John S, et al., Sci Data 2019;6:180297,
   https://zenodo.org/records/1473724), licensed CC BY 4.0. Changes made: specimen
   ALPHA's segmentations (facial nerve, chorda tympani, cochleovestibular nerve,
   scala tympani, scala vestibuli, ossicles, tympanic membrane, carotid) meshed and
   moved into the CT by a registration of the specimen's bone onto each temporal
   bone (the left one mirrored); landmarks derived from them; converted to
   compressed glTF. The greater petrosal nerve is the project's own drawing. The
   cranial nerves are Z-Anatomy's fitted courses (as in section 10, CC BY-SA)
   rerouted through the CT's foramina; the optic nerves, cervical carotids and
   jugular veins are TotalSegmentator segmentations of the CT; the sigmoid and
   transverse sinuses and the petrous carotid are traced through the CT's
   contrast-filled lumen; small arteries the CT cannot show are Z-Anatomy's
   fitted branches anchored to the CT's own parent vessels. Its surgical views
   cuts craniotomies out of the CT's bone (the project's own drawing) and shows
   the Lead-DBS MNI 2009b template surface (section 2's terms, GPL-3.0; the
   FreeSurfer labels shipped with it, research use) deformed to fit the CT, its
   right Sylvian fissure opened, its frontal lobe lifted and its right
   hemisphere held off the falx as morph targets; and Z-Anatomy's cerebellum,
   brainstem and fourth ventricle (as in section 10, CC BY-SA) fitted into the
   CT's posterior fossa, the tonsils drawn apart as a morph target.

